1.  Data Ingestion & Preprocessing
This section explains how we took the raw onboard video, why it could not be fed directly into the reconstruction model, and the exact sequence of transformations applied to make it usable.
1.1  The Raw Dataset and Why It Could Not Work
What the raw data looked like
Property	Raw Value	Problem It Causes
Resolution	1920 × 1080 pixels	Far too large for GPU pairwise processing; O(n²) pairs × full resolution = OOM
Frame rate	50 fps	Too many frames per second; motion between frames is extreme at race speed
Duration	~110 seconds	2,640+ frames; impossible to process all pairs at once in GPU memory
Car body	Bottom 40–50% of every frame	Static object confuses the model into building a 'red cylinder' artefact along the trajectory
Scale	Unknown absolute scale	Monocular video has no depth reference; scale is ambiguous without intervention
Dynamic objects	Other F1 cars visible in ~30% of frames	Moving objects corrupt the static scene reconstruction if not handled
Intrinsics	Unknown focal length / distortion	Camera matrix must be estimated rather than provided

⚠  The raw video cannot be processed directly. Every single property in the table above is a failure mode for the reconstruction model if left unaddressed. The model would either crash (OOM), produce a shapeless blob, or reconstruct a phantom car body instead of the track.
The fundamental mismatch — what the model expects
DUSt3R (the reconstruction model) is designed to receive pairs of frames where: (a) the frame resolution is approximately 512 px on the longest side, (b) the content of the frame is entirely scene geometry with no static foreground object permanently blocking half the view, and (c) consecutive frames have manageable inter-frame displacement. The raw video violates all three conditions.
1.2  The Preprocessing Pipeline — Step by Step
Step 1 — Trim to the useful segment
The raw video is 2 minutes 14 seconds. We trim to the first 1 minute 50 seconds, which covers one complete lap. The final 24 seconds contain pit-lane footage with stationary cameras — useless for trajectory reconstruction and harmful if included.
T_trim  =  t ∈ [0,  110]  seconds
Tool:  ffmpeg -t 00:01:50 -c copy  (stream copy, no re-encode — zero quality loss)
Step 2 — Spatial Downscale
We resize each frame from 1920×1080 to 1024×576 using bilinear interpolation. This is a 4× reduction in pixel count per frame, directly reducing GPU memory consumption for the subsequent frame-pair processing.
(H_out, W_out)  =  (576, 1024),    scale_factor  =  576/1080  ≈  0.533
The reconstruction network internally resizes inputs to 512 px regardless, so any resolution above 512 wide is discarded by the model anyway. Keeping 1080p only wastes I/O time and disk space.
Tool:  ffmpeg  -vf scale=1024:576  (bilinear, default ffmpeg scaler)
Step 3 — Frame Rate Reduction
We reduce the frame rate from 50 fps to 24 fps by keeping every k-th frame (k = 2, so 50/2 = 25 ≈ 24 fps). We experimented with multiple values:
fps tested	Inter-frame baseline	Result
50 fps	~1.1 m at 200 km/h	Too dense — redundant frames, slow processing
24 fps	~2.3 m at 200 km/h	✓ Optimal — good parallax, manageable motion
15 fps	~3.7 m at 200 km/h	Borderline — reconstruction quality drops slightly
10 fps	~5.6 m at 200 km/h	Fails — inter-frame motion too large for flow estimator
< 12 fps	> 4.6 m at 200 km/h	✗ Catastrophic — reconstruction completely breaks down

baseline  =  v · Δt  =  200 km/h · (1/fps seconds)  =  55.6 m/s · Δt
Hard constraint:  Never go below 12 fps for F1 footage. Below this threshold the inter-frame motion exceeds the optical flow estimator's effective range and correspondences are lost entirely.
Step 4 — Removing the Car Body (The Critical Step)
This is the most impactful preprocessing step. We tried two approaches:
Approach A (WRONG) — Masking: set the lower half of each frame to black:
I_mask(x,y)  =  I(x,y)  if  y < H/2,   else  0          ← DISCARDED
This removed the car body visually but fed the model a frame where 50% of pixels are constant zero. The model's confidence predictor correctly assigns C ≈ 0 to those pixels, so 50% of all potential reconstruction points are discarded before computation even begins. On low-texture sections (straights, tunnels) the remaining upper-half pixels also had little texture — producing zero-point chunks.
⚠  Masking failed: produced empty chunks on 30–40% of the video. The model wasted half its compute budget on black pixels.
Approach B (CORRECT) — Crop: physically remove the lower half:
I_crop(x,y)  =  I(x,y)   for   0 ≤ y < H/2          ← IMPLEMENTED
Every pixel the model now receives carries real scene information — barriers, track surface, buildings, sky. The effective reconstruction resolution doubles. The output frame is 1024×288.
✓  Cropping fixed the empty-chunk problem. Chunks 0, 1, 2 immediately produced 154k, 132k, 104k points respectively after switching from masking to cropping.
Step 5 — Frame Extraction and Binary Confidence Encoding
After video preprocessing, we extract individual frames as JPEG images at the 24fps rate. Each pixel then passes through the reconstruction network which outputs a confidence value C(u,v) ∈ ℝ⁺. This confidence is thresholded to produce a binary mask — the 0-and-1 encoding of the data:
M(u,v)  =  1   if   C(u,v)  ≥  C̄_τ,      M(u,v)  =  0   otherwise
where C̄_τ is the τ-th percentile of all confidence values in the chunk. Only pixels where M = 1 contribute 3D points to the reconstruction. This is the fundamental binary encoding in the pipeline — converting continuous confidence scores into a binary keep/discard decision for every pixel.
τ (percentile)	Points kept	Effect on output
0.5 (top 50%)	Fewest points	High precision, many empty chunks on low-texture sections
0.3 (top 70%)	Moderate	Good balance for textured sections
0.0 (all points)	All points	Maximises coverage; noise in sky/uniform regions
Final choice: 0.0 + voxel filter	All, then downsampled	Keeps everything, removes spatial duplicates

Step 6 — Overlapping Chunks (Memory Management)
The processed video is split into overlapping windows. This is the memory management step: it makes the otherwise-impossible (2,640-frame, full-GPU) problem tractable by dividing it into small pieces, while the overlap ensures the pieces can be stitched back together.
C_j  =  { f_{j·s},  f_{j·s+1},  …,  f_{j·s+c−1} },   stride  s = c − o  =  20 − 5  =  15
The o = 5 overlapping frames between consecutive chunks are the stitching anchors used in Section 3.
1.3  What the Data Looked Like After Preprocessing
After the full preprocessing pipeline the data entering the model has these properties:
●	Frame size — 512×144 pixels (DUSt3R's internal resize of our 1024×288 crop)
●	Frame rate — 24 fps effective
●	Content — 100% valid scene pixels — no black regions, no car body
●	Binary encoding — Each pixel will be assigned M ∈ {0, 1} by the confidence threshold
●	Chunk count — ~88 chunks of 20 frames each, 5-frame overlap
●	Memory per chunk — Fits in 4–16 GB VRAM depending on GPU
✓  The data is now in the correct form for the model. Every pixel the network sees is a real scene pixel. The binary confidence mask will cleanly separate high-quality geometry from noise.
2.  Formula Analysis & Constraints
This section provides a detailed analysis of every mathematical formula used in the VROOM pipeline, the constraints applied at each stage, and what the data looks like at each formula's output.
2.1  Formula 1 — Pairwise Pointmap Prediction
The formula
For any two frames I_a and I_b from the same chunk, the DUSt3R network f computes:
( X_a,  X_b,  C_a,  C_b )  =  f_θ( I_a,  I_b )
where θ are the learned network weights (fixed after training — we do not retrain). X_a ∈ ℝ^{H×W×3} is the pointmap: the predicted 3D coordinates of the scene point visible at every pixel (u,v) of frame I_a, expressed in I_a's camera coordinate system. X_b is the same but for I_b, also expressed in I_a's frame — this is the key property that makes explicit feature matching unnecessary.
Constraints on this formula
●	Input size — Frames must be exactly 512 px on the longest side (enforced by the network's patch embedding layer). Non-square inputs are padded internally.
●	Pair count — For n frames, we create n(n-1)/2 symmetric pairs. Maximum n ≈ 12–15 on a 16 GB GPU before OOM.
●	Confidence range — C(u,v) ∈ (1, ∞) after the exponential activation: conf_mode = ('exp', 1, ∞). A pixel with C = 1 has minimum confidence; higher = more certain.
●	Depth range — X values are unbounded in theory; in practice points beyond ~100m from the camera are near-zero confidence and filtered.
Data after this formula
The output is a set of dense pointmaps — one per frame — and their confidence maps. These are raw per-pair predictions, each in a different local coordinate frame. They cannot yet be combined into a coherent point cloud; that requires the next formula.
2.2  Formula 2 — Global Alignment Energy
The formula
Global alignment fuses all per-pair pointmaps into one consistent reconstruction by solving an optimisation problem. We seek camera poses T_i ∈ SE(3), per-pair scales σ_{ij}, and canonical points χ(u,v) that minimise:
E(T, σ, χ)  =  Σ_{(i,j) ∈ pairs}  Σ_{(u,v) ∈ pixels}  C^{ij}(u,v) · ‖ T_i · ( σ_{ij} · X^{ij}(u,v) ) − χ(u,v) ‖²
The rigid transform T_i acts on homogeneous coordinates:
T_i  =  [ R_i  |  t_i ;  0  |  1 ] ∈ SE(3),   T_i · p  =  R_i p + t_i
Constraints on this formula
●	Rotation constraint — R_i ∈ SO(3): det(R_i) = 1, R_i^T R_i = I. The optimiser maintains this via the manifold parameterisation of rotation.
●	Scale positivity — σ_{ij} > 0 always (enforced by parameterising as exp(·)).
●	Confidence weighting — C^{ij}(u,v) acts as a per-pixel weight. Low-confidence pixels (C ≈ 1) contribute negligibly; high-confidence pixels dominate.
●	Optimiser — Adam with cosine learning rate decay: lr(k) = 0.01 · (1 + cos(πk/300)) / 2. Runs for 300 iterations.
●	Initialisation — Minimum Spanning Tree of the pair-confidence graph: highest-confidence pairs establish the initial skeleton before lower-confidence pairs refine it.
The critical constraint — why this step cannot be skipped
⚠  If E is not minimised (i.e. if raw pointmaps are concatenated directly), every pointmap sits in its own disconnected local frame. All frames collapse to the origin. The result is a featureless blob — the primary bug in our initial implementation.
Data after this formula
The output is: (a) a set of camera poses {T_i} placing each frame in one consistent world frame, and (b) a unified point cloud χ covering the chunk's scene. The loss value E/(number of pairs) is the quality metric — values below 0.010 indicate good alignment; above 0.020 indicates drift or degeneracy.
✓  After alignment: chunk_000 loss = 0.0064, chunk_001 loss = 0.0005, chunk_002 loss = 0.0005. All three produced 100k+ points with clear track geometry.
2.3  Formula 3 — Binary Confidence Filter
The formula
After alignment, we apply the binary mask from Section 1.3:
P_keep  =  {  p_k  :  C_k  ≥  Quantile_τ ( C_{chunk} )  }
M_k  =  1  if  C_k ≥ C̄_τ,     M_k = 0  otherwise
Constraints
●	τ = 0.0 (final setting) — All points kept; downstream voxel downsampling removes spatial noise.
●	Minimum cluster size — Chunks producing < 10 points total are skipped — flagged as degenerate (empty chunks from fast straights / tunnels).
●	Finite value check — Points where any coordinate is NaN or ±∞ are excluded regardless of confidence.
Data after this formula
A filtered point cloud P_keep ⊂ ℝ³ and corresponding confidence vector C_keep ∈ ℝ^{|P_keep|}. The binary mask M is the direct 0-and-1 encoding referred to in the WBS template — every point is either kept (1) or discarded (0).
2.4  Formula 4 — Chunk Stitching (Rigid Weld)
The formula
To place chunk j+1 into the global frame, we use the shared overlap frames between chunk j (already in the global frame) and chunk j+1 (in its own local frame):
T_align  =  T_j^{last_overlap}  ·  ( T_{j+1}^{first_overlap} )^{−1}
where (·)^{−1} is the inverse of a SE(3) matrix: T^{−1} = [R^T | −R^T t ; 0 | 1]. Every point p in chunk j+1 is then mapped to the global frame:
p_global  =  R_align · p_local  +  t_align,   where  T_align = (R_align, t_align)
Constraints
●	Orthogonality — R_align must be a valid rotation. Computed directly from two valid SE(3) matrices, so this is guaranteed.
●	Chain rule — Each chunk is welded to the previous one sequentially. Error accumulates — this is the source of drift.
●	Fallback — If a chunk has too few valid poses (< 3), the previous transform is reused (identity scale, same R and t).
2.5  Formula 5 — Umeyama Similarity Transform (Scale-Aware Stitching)
The formula
When per-chunk scale varies, we solve for a similarity transform (s, R, t) using n corresponding camera centres from the overlap:
min_{s, R, t}   (1/n) Σᵢ  ‖  yᵢ  −  (s R xᵢ + t)  ‖²
Closed-form solution via SVD of the cross-covariance:
Σ_{xy}  =  (1/n) Σᵢ (yᵢ − ȳ)(xᵢ − x̄)ᵀ  =  U D Vᵀ
R  =  U S Vᵀ,      S  =  diag(1, 1, det(U)·det(V))
s  =  tr(D S) / σ²_x,      t  =  ȳ  −  s R x̄,      σ²_x  =  (1/n)Σ‖xᵢ−x̄‖²
Constraints
●	Minimum overlap — n ≥ 3 camera centres required; below this SVD is degenerate.
●	SVD convergence — Protected by try/except — if SVD fails (collinear cameras), fall back to rigid weld.
●	Scale positivity — s > 0 guaranteed when σ²_x > 0 (non-degenerate point set) and tr(DS) > 0.
●	Reflection guard — S = diag(1,1,det(U)det(V)) ensures det(R) = +1, preventing an improper rotation.
Data after this formula
A similarity-aligned chunk whose scale is normalised relative to the previous chunk. The scale correction factor s is logged per chunk — values far from 1.0 indicate chunks where the reconstruction scale drifted significantly.
