3.  Model Architecture & Implementation
This section identifies the machine learning models used, explains exactly how they work, details the hyperparameters and constraints, and describes how the binary-masked, preprocessed data flows through to the final 3D output.
3.1  The Models — DUSt3R and MonST3R
Why these models and not classical alternatives
Approach	Method	Failure on F1 footage	Why
Classical	ORB-SLAM / COLMAP	Fails at high speed	Keypoint matching fails when features blur across frames
Classical	LSD-SLAM	Fails on low texture	Line-based SLAM needs strong edges; tunnels have none
Learned	SuperPoint + SuperGlue	Partially works	Better matching but still requires explicit correspondence
Learned	DUSt3R / MonST3R	Works	No explicit matching; full-image attention reasons about scene geometry globally

DUSt3R Architecture
DUSt3R is a Siamese Vision Transformer. Both input frames pass through a shared ViT-Large encoder (24 transformer layers, embedding dimension 1024, 16 attention heads). The encoder converts each frame into a sequence of 16×16-pixel patch tokens.
A cross-attention decoder then processes both token sequences simultaneously, allowing patches in frame A to attend to all patches in frame B and vice versa. This cross-frame attention is what enables the model to reason about 3D structure without explicit keypoint matching — it effectively performs dense stereo matching implicitly through attention.
Two output heads produce the pointmaps X_a, X_b and confidence maps C_a, C_b. The depth output uses an exponential activation to enforce positivity:
X(u,v)  =  exp( F_decoder(u,v) ),   C(u,v)  =  exp( G_decoder(u,v) ) + 1
The +1 in the confidence activation ensures C ≥ 1 always, consistent with the constraint table in Section 2.6.
MonST3R Extension
MonST3R adds a dense optical flow input alongside the image pair. Optical flow F_{a→b}(u,v) ∈ ℝ² gives the pixel displacement from frame a to frame b for every pixel. The flow is computed by a separate network (RAFT-Large, pre-trained) and fed to MonST3R as an additional conditioning signal.
MonST3R additionally outputs per-pixel motion masks M_a, M_b ∈ [0,1]:
( X_a, X_b, C_a, C_b, M_a, M_b )  =  f_{MonST3R}( I_a, I_b, F_{a→b} )
Pixels where M ≈ 1 (moving objects — other F1 cars) are down-weighted in the global alignment energy E. This prevents moving cars from being reconstructed into the static track point cloud.
