3.3  How the Model Processed the 0-and-1 Data
The binary data (confidence mask M ∈ {0,1} per pixel) interacts with the model in two places:
During global alignment — binary weighting
The confidence values C(u,v) produced by the model are continuous, but after thresholding they become binary: M(u,v) ∈ {0,1}. In the global alignment energy E, only pixels where M = 1 contribute:
E  =  Σ_{(i,j)}  Σ_{(u,v): M^{ij}(u,v)=1}  C^{ij}(u,v) · ‖ T_i · (σ_{ij} X^{ij}(u,v)) − χ(u,v) ‖²
Pixels with M = 0 are excluded from the sum entirely. This is the direct use of the binary encoding: it acts as a hard gate that prevents low-confidence pixels from influencing the pose estimation at all.
During point cloud construction — binary selection
After alignment, only M = 1 pixels become 3D points in the output cloud:
P_output  =  { χ(u,v)  :  M(u,v) = 1 }
M = 0 pixels are simply not included. This is the binary-to-3D conversion: the confidence threshold converts the continuous pointmap into a discrete point cloud by binary selection.
